<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Masnavi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Masnavi-ye Ma'navi, Mathnawi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Masnavi, or Masnavi-ye Ma'navi, is a long Persian poem by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Jalal al-Din Rumi</a>, composed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> in the last years of his life. In six books of some 25,000 rhyming couplets, it teaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> doctrine through stories, fables and commentary on the Qur'an. It is one of the most influential works of Persian literature and of Islamic mysticism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/masnavi.jpg" alt="Masnavi" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georges Jansoone (JoJan) · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkey.Konya049.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a> began dictating the Masnavi in the final years of his life, at around the age of 54, about the year 1258, and kept composing verses until his death in 1273. It is documented that he began at the request of his favourite disciple, Husam al-Din Chalabi, who observed that many of Rumi's followers dutifully read the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanai">Sana'i</a> and '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athtar">Attar</a>. Rumi therefore created a work in their didactic style to complement his other poetry. The inconclusive ending of the sixth book has led some to suggest that the work was unfinished at Rumi's death, and others to claim that another volume exists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The title Masnavi-ye Ma'navi means The Spiritual Couplets. The work is a series of six books amounting to around 25,000 verses, or 50,000 lines, each book of about 4,000 verses with its own prose introduction and prologue. It collects anecdotes and stories drawn from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> and everyday tales, and each story illustrates a point whose moral is then discussed in detail. It has no framed plot, and its scenes range from the local bazaar to fables and tales of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a>'s time. In contrast to Rumi's Divan-i Shams-i Tabrizi, it is a relatively sober text that explains the dimensions of spiritual life and practice to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> disciples.</p><p>The books fall into three pairs. Books 1 and 2 are chiefly concerned with the nafs, the lower carnal self, and its self-deception. Books 3 and 4 share the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> of Reason and Knowledge, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a> personifies in the figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>. Books 5 and 6 are joined by the ideal that a person must deny physical earthly existence to understand God's existence. Rumi speaks in several voices, among them the authority of a teacher, the storyteller, the analogist and the characters of dialogue, and he sometimes questions his own verses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Masnavi is one of the most influential works in the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a>. It has sometimes been described as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> in Persian, and some Muslims regard it as among the most important works of Islamic literature, behind only the Quran. Many commentators have viewed it as the finest mystical poem in world literature. R. A. Nicholson edited it from the oldest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, with the first complete English translation, in eight volumes. Jawid Mojaddedi's translation for Oxford World's Classics is the first complete one in English verse, its sixth book published in 2025.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Masnavi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rumi, who died there in 1273, wrote the <b>Masnavi</b>, one of the most influential works of Persian poetry, and his followers formed the Mevlevi order of whirling dervishes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…and Khosrow and Shirin; Attar wrote the Conference of the Birds, an allegory of the mystical path; Saadi wrote the Gulistan, “The Rose Garden”, in prose and verse; and Rumi, who died in 1273, composed the <b>Masnavi</b>, an immense poem of mystical teaching, and thousands of lyric poems.</blockquote><a href="/language/persian">Persian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
